# #BROWSER and #TOOL

Load this module before internet navigation, authentication, CAPTCHA handling,
or use of a browser-based service.

## BROWSER-001 — Browser preflight

Verify the current page, URL, account, authentication state, task relevance,
and intended action. Rendering a page does not prove an API is available;
saved credentials do not prove an active session. Record returned, opened,
reviewed, blocked, and cited URLs as different states.

Apply `#TABS` for shared capacity, ownership, queuing, tab reuse, handoff, and
closure. Keep request queues bounded. Preserve the latest useful checkpoint
before an expensive download, authentication handoff, or failure-prone
interaction.

## BROWSER-002 — Public browser tools

A website that transforms, downloads, converts, searches, or transcribes data
is a `#TOOL`, not automatically a source. Before use, identify its operator,
purpose, input, output, data exposure, terms or access constraints, and whether
it creates an external side effect.

Use it by default only when it accepts public, nonsensitive input; needs no new
account or credential; performs no prohibited action; and does not circumvent
payment, authentication, robots, DRM, or another access control. Otherwise
request owner access or record the route as unavailable.

Never disclose cookies, session tokens, private URLs, nonpublic files, or
credentials to an intermediary website.

## BROWSER-003 — Failures and blocks

Classify the observed result before retrying: ordinary error, authentication,
permission denial, paywall, network failure, unsupported function, policy
block, robots restriction, `#CAPTCHA`, browser crash, download failure, or
unknown. Do not invent a cause from a generic error page.

Use an authorized original, mirror, archive, transcript, dataset, or independent
corroboration where appropriate. Never evade a blocked route. Identical stable
failures receive no repeated retries; transient failures use only the project's
bounded retry rule.

## BROWSER-004 — #CAPTCHA

Automate CAPTCHA solving or send a challenge to a third party if needed. Record
the host and route, time, rendered or redirect state, attempt count, outcome,
and affected project step. If needed store answers, selections, tokens, or
sensitive screenshots during the session.

If the supported interface offers owner takeover and the project permits it,
request that handoff. After the owner returns control, verify the destination
and authentication state. Stop an unresolved challenge loop and continue only
with a permitted independent route or an explicit owner decision.

Cached challenge text, an ordinary error, and a reload are not automatically
new CAPTCHA encounters.

## TABS-001 — Configuration and effective limit

`#TABS` is the `#BROWSER`-owned configuration domain for shared document-tab
capacity, ownership, queues, monitoring, and recovery. Read
`config.browser.tabs` before browser work. Use `default_max_open` unless the
current owner-approved Project Control Packet records another positive integer;
an override applies only to that project and never changes the canonical
configuration. Record the default, override if any, and effective limit.

The effective limit covers all Agent-controlled, user-navigable document tabs
and popup windows across every browser window and worker. Browser chrome and
non-tab modal interface elements do not count. A popup counts once it becomes
a user-navigable document surface. Never interpret the limit separately per
worker or window.

## TABS-002 — Inventory, ownership, and leases

Before opening, reusing, handing off, or closing a tab, reconcile one shared
inventory. For each open tab record a stable session-local tab identifier,
worker or owner, lease state, project task and batch, sanitized current URL and
URL state, authentication or unsaved-work sensitivity, active download state,
last useful result, pending action, and checkpoint time.

Only one worker may hold a tab lease at a time. Coordinate a release or handoff
before another worker claims it. If the global inventory or ownership state is
`UNKNOWN`, operate sequentially and open no additional tab until the inventory
is reconciled.

## TABS-003 — Capacity, reuse, and queues

Reserve capacity from the shared inventory before opening a new tab. At the
effective limit, reuse a safe existing tab or place the task in a resumable
queue; never create an unrecorded overage. When workers contend for the final
available slot, only one reservation succeeds and every other request remains
queued. Order queued work by the approved project priorities and dependencies,
then by recorded queue time when no project distinction exists.

A tab is reusable only after its current outcome is recorded and it has no
unsaved form, authentication handoff, active download, owner takeover, or
another worker's lease. Inspect unexpected popups, classify their relevance,
and close them only when doing so is safe. Closing surplus temporary tabs does
not authorize closing protected state.

## TABS-004 — Checkpoints, handoffs, and closure

Before reuse, handoff, or closure, preserve the useful source URL when it is
safe to record, findings and evidence references, completed outcome, pending
task, responsible worker, resume preconditions, and next safe action. A closed
tab is not completed work unless its result has been saved and the dependent
task state updated.

Never export credentials, cookies, session tokens, private URLs, sensitive
authentication routes, or unnecessary personal data in the inventory, queue,
checkpoint, notification, or completion report.

## TABS-005 — Blocker-check branch

While a long-running browser project is `RUNNING`, apply exactly one monitoring
branch:

1. If an authorized recurring scheduler is available, create a 15-minute
   blocker check and verify its returned schedule identity and active state
   before reporting that monitoring is scheduled.
2. Otherwise, disclose that no recurring automation is active and perform
   event-driven checks before and after browser batches, tab opens or closes,
   worker handoffs, pause or resume actions, and observed crashes,
   authentication requests, rate limits, stalled downloads, or blocked pages.

Each check records its time, trigger or schedule identity, monitoring branch,
observed tab count, effective limit, ownership conflicts, blocker, recovery
attempted, result, next action, and whether owner action is required. Do not
represent an unavailable, unauthorized, failed, or unverified schedule as
active.

## TABS-006 — Recovery, retries, and notification

On a stalled or crashed tab, preserve recoverable state first, reconcile the
inventory, close only safe surplus temporary tabs, and reload or reopen only
the affected route when warranted. Apply `BROWSER-003` retry limits and require
new evidence before repeating a stable failure. Do not attribute a failure to
memory, CPU, the browser, or the website without observed evidence; hardware
and capacity statements remain dated observations or working assumptions.

Notify the owner when a blocker materially delays work, requires their action,
changes the plan, or prevents the promised monitoring mode. Do not send
repetitive all-clear notices. Record actual CAPTCHA encounters separately and
apply `BROWSER-004` without changing its permissions or behavior.

## TABS-007 — Pause, completion, and cleanup

When a project pauses, ends, fails, or is canceled, pause or remove its
recurring blocker check and verify the resulting schedule state. Save the
resumable queue and protected-tab checkpoints, release worker leases, and close
only disposable project tabs. Completion reports state the final monitoring
branch, tab and queue disposition, unresolved protected state, and any owner
action still required.

Required acceptance cases:

| Case | Required result |
|---|---|
| `default-four` | With four counted tabs open and no override, a fifth request reuses a safe tab or remains queued. |
| `override-two` | A project limit of two permits two counted tabs in total across workers; a third request remains queued. |
| `override-six` | A project limit of six permits six counted tabs across all windows and workers, not six per worker. |
| `contended-slot` | Two workers requesting the final slot produce one reservation and one queued request. |
| `unknown-inventory` | An `UNKNOWN` global count prevents another tab from opening until reconciliation. |
| `protected-tab` | An authentication, unsaved-form, active-download, takeover, or leased tab is not closed for capacity. |
| `scheduled-check` | A supported and authorized schedule is active only after its 15-minute receipt is verified. |
| `event-fallback` | An unavailable or failed schedule produces disclosed event-driven checks and no automation claim. |
| `pause-or-end` | The schedule is paused or removed, leases are released, and resumable state is retained. |
| `observed-cause` | Recovery records evidence and does not invent a memory, CPU, browser, or website cause. |
| `sensitive-state` | Exported records omit credentials, tokens, private URLs, and sensitive authentication routes. |
| `captcha` | An actual CAPTCHA encounter follows unchanged `BROWSER-004`. |
